"Prioritizes explicit assumptions by their importance to success and the strength of supporting evidence.\n\nUse when: Choose the next useful experiment for a business idea. Reveal confident claims that rest on little direct evidence.\n\nAvoid when: The task is comparing implementation effort or estimating operational risk frequency.\n\nVisual grammar: Two axes represent assumption importance and evidence strength. Cards contain precise, testable statements. Evidence references and risk categories provide context without changing axis meaning.\n\nBuild: Extract what must be true for the idea to work. Rewrite assumptions as discrete claims and assess existing evidence. Prioritize important weakly supported claims for focused tests.\n\nCheck: Evidence means relevant observations rather than team confidence. Each claim is precise enough for a useful test. Low-evidence, high-importance placement has an explicit rationale.\n\nPitfalls: Mapping features or solutions instead of assumptions. Treating many weak anecdotes as strong evidence.\n\nExample: A delivery venture maps assumptions about repeat demand, driver availability and payment willingness, selecting the least-supported critical claim first.\n\nAlternatives: Test card, Opportunity solution tree."
